2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hsiao-ch'in Hsien Huang-hou''' 孝欽顯皇后 née '''Yehe Nara''' ('''Yehonala''') 葉赫那拉, Nov. 29, 1835-1908, Nov. 15, is referred to in these biographies as '''Empress Hsiao-ch'in''', but was also known by her title, as '''Tz'û-hsi Huan
2 CONC g-t'ai-hou''' 慈禧皇太后, or by her residence as '''Hsi T'ai-hou''' 西太后. In Western works she is generally known as the '''Empress Dowager''' or "The Old Buddha", the latter a translation of the Chinese term, "Lao Fo-yeh" 老佛爺. In the P
2 CONC alace she was sometimes referred to as "Lao Tsu-tsung" 老祖宗, or "Venerable Ancestor", in reference to her position in later life as head of the Imperial Family. Her own family belonged to the Manchu Bordered Blue Banner. Her father
2 CONC , [https://www.geni.com/people/HZ/6000000009796776788 Hui-chêng 惠徵] (posthumous name 端恪), was a clerk in the Board of Civil Office, at least during the years 1836 to 1837. He later rose to be intendant of the Circuit of Southern A
2 CONC nhwei (Hui-Ning-Ch'ih-T'ai Tao 徽寧池太道) but was cashiered in 1853 for leaving his post in the face of the advancing Taiping forces. He seems to have died soon after. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851 [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in], then seventeen ''sui'', was selected to enter the Palace as a low ranking concubine of [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emp
2 CONC eror Wên-tsung] (i.e., I-chu q.v.). Her status was raised in 1854, and again in 1856 when she gave birth to a son, [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un] [q.v.], and was made a second class concubine with 
2 CONC the title, I Fei 懿妃. Since her son was the only heir of [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung], and therefore destined to succeed the throne, her position in the Palace was greatly strengthene
2 CONC d. In later years she confided to [https://www.geni.com/people/QHJ/6000000020310998695 Ch'ü Hung-chi] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SJN/6000000011635009704 Sun Chia-nai]) that she learned about affairs of state from [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung] who let her classify his memorials for him. In 1860, when the British and French allies advanced on Peking she and the child, then five ''sui'', accompanied 
2 CONC the [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor] to Jehôl. On August 21, 1861, the day before the [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor] died, the child was proclaimed Heir Apparen
2 CONC t. During his minority affairs of state were entrusted, according to his father's will, to a regency composed of eight men, including four adjutant generals, and four Grand Councilors, then in Jehôl (see under [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun]). In issuing decrees, however, the eight regents were to obtain the consent of the Dowager Empresses, namely, the child emperor's mother, and the senior consort, [https://www.geni.com/peopl
2 CONC e/XZ/6000000009796838324 Empress Hsiao-chên 孝貞顯皇后] (née Niuhuru 鈕祜祿, 1837-1881), also known as Tz'û-an (慈安) Huang T'ai-hou, or Tung (東) T'ai-hou. Before his death the [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emper
2 CONC or] is said to have entrusted to [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] a seal bearing the characters Yü-shang 御賞 to be impressed at the beginning of every edict issued by the regency. To [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un], in whose name the edicts would be issued, was given a seal bearing the characters T'ung-tao-t'ang 同道堂, to be impressed at the close of each edict. But as the child was still in his m
2 CONC inority this seal was entrusted to his mother. Hence without her consent to use the seal no edict could be issued or recognized as authentic. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the first edicts issued by the regency from Jehôl, in the name of the child Emperor ([https://www.geni.com/people/TZ6000000008737146186 Mu-tsung]), raised both [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên]
2 CONC  and [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] to the rank of Huang-t'ai-hou, or Dowager Empresses—the former with the designation Mu Hou 母后 "Empress Mother", the latter with the designation, Shêng Mu 聖母 "Sa
2 CONC intly Mother". Another edict ordered [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] [q.v.], half-brother of the deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor], to look after affairs in Pek
2 CONC ing—the Allied Forces having by then departed and foreign ministers having taken up residence there. Early in September 1861 [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] went to Jehôl, with [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/WX/6000000049172096927 Wên-hsiang] [qq.v.], to persuade the Court to return to Peking and perhaps also to find out how the regency was functioning. What took 
2 CONC place at the conference is not known, but in view of subsequent events the visit foreshadowed a series of acts which led to the overthrow of the regency. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About this time a censor was memorialized, recommending that [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Empress Hsiao-chên] should become regent to rule with the assistance not only of the eight men then in power but with
2 CONC  the help of one or two princes closely related by blood to the deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor]. On September 14, 1861 the eight co-regents drafted a decree rebuking the censor and remin
2 CONC ding him that precedents of the dynasty forbade the elevation of an empress to supreme control of the Empire. The Empresses, of course, disapproved this draft decree, and for some time refused to impress their seals upon it; but t
2 CONC he co-regents forced them to do so by holding up all matters of state until it was issued. The Dowager Empresses summoned [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] [q.v.], husband of [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]'s sister, to an audience in Jehôl, but the co-regents refused to report his arrival, compelling him to wait outside the Palace, though he had come all the way from Peking. Finally, with the help
2 CONC  of a eunuch, [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] obtained the audience at which it seems a plan was drafted for the removal of the eight co-regents. The necessary military support came from General Shêng-p
2 CONC ao (see under Lin Fêng-hsiang) who on September 17 or 18 published a memorial denouncing the eight co-regents. Soon after, a date was set for the return of the Court to Peking and also for the transfer of [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung]'s remains to that city. The Court left Jehôl on October 26 and entered the capital five days later. Immediately a decree was issued blaming the regents for having brought on the 
2 CONC calamitous war with the Allied Forces in 1860; for having wrongly counselled [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung] to remain at Jehôl, and so contributing to his death; and for having forced 
2 CONC the Empresses to issue the edict condemning the above-mentioned censor. Seven regents who had returned to Peking were arrested by [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], and the eighth, [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun] [q.v.], who was escorting the deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor]'s coffin, was arrested north of Peking by a detachment of cavalry under [https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]. On November 8, [https://www.geni.com/people/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun] was beheaded and the other seven regents were punished (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SS/600000000
2 CONC 9823430303 Su-shun]). The Dowager Empresses, [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] and [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in], formed a joint regency, known as Ch'ui-lien t'ing-c
2 CONC hêng 垂簾聽政 ("Listening from behind Screens to Reports on Governmental Affairs"). [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was made Prince Counselor (議政王) to advise the regents on all state affairs. At this time [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] had very little power, but was included in the regency, probably because her knowledge of written Chinese was useful to the senior [https://www.geni.com/people/Ci-An/
2 CONC 6000000009796838324 Dowager Empress]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was deprived of his special status in 1865, and thereafter the regency of the Dowager Empresses increased in power. By entrusting military powers to such able Chinese as 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan], [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang] [qq.v.], the campai
2 CONC gns against the Taipings and other rebels were carried to a victorious conclusion. The Taiping Rebellion was crushed in 1864, the bandits of the northern provinces were pacified in 1868 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ
2 CONC /6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in]), and the Muslim uprising in Yunnan and Kweichow was put down in 1873 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/CYY/6000000020170521894 Ts'ên Yü-ying]). International affairs were entrusted to
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], [https://www.geni.com/people/WX/6000000049172096927 Wên-hsiang] and other members of the Tsung-li Yamen (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I
2 CONC -hsin]). The gradual Westernization of China began at this time with the establishment of schools of foreign languages at Canton, Shanghai and Peking; the opening of a Navy Yard at Foochow; the creation of arsenals at Shanghai, Na
2 CONC nking and other cities; and the formation of a modern Customs Service, with the help of foreign experts. The Russian occupation of Ili in 1870 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang] and [h
2 CONC ttps://www.geni.com/people/CH/6000000011325508386 Ch'ung-hou]) and the Japanese invasion of Formosa in 1874 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SBZ/6000000026592944887 Shên Pao-chên]) were events that strained relations with f
2 CONC oreign countries, but in general the successes of the regency caused the T'ung chih reign-period to be known in history as one of national revival (中興). 
2 CONT
2 CONT As [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] was neither able nor ambitious, [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] gradually assumed more responsibility in appointing offici
2 CONC als to important posts and in determining national policies. She won the loyalty of these officials and through them controlled the state. Inside the Palace she ruled through her favorite eunuchs. The execution of her chief eunuch
2 CONC , An Tê-hai, in 1869 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/DBZ/6000000019543624955 Ting Pao-chên]), was a set-back to her authority, and she suspected [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] and [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] of plotting his downfall and death. She is said to have turned against [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] for alienating the affections of [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un] and for siding with the latter in his choice of a wife. Being his mother, [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] naturally regarded the selection of a 
2 CONC daughter-in-law as her own prerogative. After [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un]'s marriage to [https://www.geni.com/people/Xiaozhe/6000000009824530504 Empress Hsiao-chê] (see under [https://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un]) in 1872 and the termination of the regency in 1873, he was still bound by the rules of filial piety to obey his mother, with the result that her control over state affairs continued al
2 CONC most unabated. Rumors arose that she thwarted him in his companionship with his wife, that she encouraged him in various excesses, and that from these excesses he finally died. Some biographers aver that the immediate cause of his
2 CONC  death was a shock received one day when his mother in anger unexpectedly appeared as he was talking confidentially with his wife. Since reports of this nature are manifestly difficult to confirm we are left with the official stat
2 CONC ement that he died of smallpox (January 12, 1875). He left no heir. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 18, 1874, because of [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un]'s illness, the Dowager Empresses again became regents. On the afternoon of his death [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777
2 CONC 603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in], having made certain that troops favorable to her were in complete control of the city, called, with [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên]'s nominal consent, a meeting of the Prin
2 CONC ces, Grand Councilors, Ministers of the Household, and Tutors and Secretaries of the deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor]. Though the Empresses presided jointly, it was [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] who assumed the leadership and opened the conference by asking the group whether or not a return to rule by Ch'ui-lien t'ing-chêng was desirable. Noting no obstinate objections, she decla
2 CONC red herself opposed to the placing of a mature person on the throne, and in favor of selecting a child amenable to education, for the duties that would later be his. She then informed the Assembly that she and [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] had already agreed upon the three-year-old child, [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] [q.v.], eldest son of [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/600000000979654684
2 CONC 1 I-huan], as their choice. The announcement came as a surprise to all present—even to [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan], the father of the child in question. It was a flagrant violation of the dynastic l
2 CONC aw of succession which provided that in case of adoption the one chosen should belong to the next generation below the deceased, in order not to throw into confusion the relative positions of the Imperial Family group. If, however
2 CONC , she had chosen, as the regulations required, a nephew of the deceased, she would have been compelled to yield her position as Empress Dowager to [https://www.geni.com/people/Xiaozhe/6000000009824530504 Empress Hsiao-chê] whom sh
2 CONC e disliked and had treated with cruelty, and from whom she would have every reason to fear reprisals if placed under her authority. [https://www.geni.com/people/Xiaozhe/6000000009824530504 Empress Hsiao-chê], on the other hand, un
2 CONC derstood clearly the precariousness of her position under the new arrangement. Seeing no hope for herself, or (as some sources maintain) for her unborn child, she committed suicide (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/600000
2 CONC 0008737146186 Tsai-ch'un]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT As soon as the Council agreed to the adoption of [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien], [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] ordered the child to be brought from [https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]'s home to the Palace. Although it was midnight, the proper imperial costumes for the child-emperor were promptly produced, indicating that the decision had been made some time previous
2 CONC ly. The child, later known as [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung], was escorted through the streets by the soldiers under [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] [q.v.] and en
2 CONC tered the Palace early in the morning of the following day. He became Emperor with the reign-title, Kuang-hsü (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]), meaning "Glorious Succession", and the two 
2 CONC Dowager Empresses again became co-regents. A few courtiers ventured to object to the choice, on the ground the [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un] was thus left without an heir, but [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] soon took steps to silence such criticism by issuing an edict, promising that as soon as [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] had a male heir th
2 CONC at son would become the adopted son of [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un], and would continue his line. Opposition, nevertheless, continued, and when four years later Wu K'o-tu [q.v.] committed suicide
2 CONC  near [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Tsai-ch'un]'s grave, so great was the storm aroused that [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] was forced to re-affirm her solemn promise that th
2 CONC e deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor] would not be left without heirs to worship at his tomb. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1880 [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] became ill, and [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] acted as sole regent for about a year. On April 7, 1881, [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] died suddenly, after only a day's illness. Rumors spread that she had been poisoned by [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in], but the real cause 
2 CONC of her death will probably never be known. In deference to her more able and far more aggressive co-regent, [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên] had gradually relinquished much of the power which as wife
2 CONC  and Empress of [https://www.geni.com/people/Xianfeng/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung] were rightfully hers, and now her life, too, was forfeited. Left as sole regent, [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empr
2 CONC ess Hsiao-ch'in] at once took steps to consolidate her authority. Long dissatisfied with [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], probably because of his hand in the execution of An Tê-hai, she finally displace
2 CONC d him in 1884. Taking advantage of the attack of certain irresponsible censors upon him for his conciliatory attitude toward France over the question of Annam, she had him deprived of all his posts and cleverly divided his offices
2 CONC  among the following Princes: [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan], father of the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor], was ordered to be consulted on all national affairs; [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 Prince Ch'ing] (I-k'uang, see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]) was entrusted with affairs at the Tsung-li Yamen; and [https://www.geni.com/people/SD/600
2 CONC 0000009823407697 Prince Li] (Shih-to, see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZL/6000000009822745089 Chao-lien]) was placed in charge of the Grand Council. Control of affairs inside the Palace was placed in the hands of several eun
2 CONC uchs. For advice on matters outside the capital she relied chiefly on the seasoned statesman, [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang]. When the Office of Naval Affairs was established in 1885 it was ent
2 CONC rusted to [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] and [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang], with two assistants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1886 [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] inspected the new navy, accompanied by [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]'s favorite eunuch, Li Lien-ying 李蓮英 (d. 1911). In the cour
2 CONC se of the tour a censor reprimanded the eunuch for insolence, and warned of the dangers of eunuch rule, but [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] defended the culprit, and the censor was degraded. In the same
2 CONC  year [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] led the courtiers in entreating the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress] to continue her regency and not to transfer the government to [http
2 CONC s://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] as previously promised. After a proper show of reluctance, the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress] consented and ruled as regent two years l
2 CONC onger. Finally, on March 4, 1889, six days after the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] had married her niece, a daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796573009 Kuei-hsiang] (see under [ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009824088033 I-tsung]), she relinquished nominal control, doubtless, however, counting on her niece to help direct the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor], or at least t
2 CONC o report matters to her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereupon [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] retired to the Summer Palace northwest of Peking, known as I Ho Yüan 頤和園, or Wan Shou Shan 萬壽山. In the years 1886-91 this Palace was reconstructed 
2 CONC from an old imperial garden, Ch'ing-i Yüan 清漪園, which had been partially destroyed by the British and French forces in 1860. The rebuilding was financed by funds intended for the construction of a navy (see under [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]). Though a masterpiece of landscape gardening, its construction deprived the country of a much-needed navy. At this retreat the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress
2 CONC  Dowager] each year inspected the troops on parade. But the expense for building and maintaining it was enormous, so that aside from large drafts on the treasury, she had to invent her own sources of revenue. High officials paid t
2 CONC o her large sums for brief audiences and minor officials paid for promotions—the power of appointment having never been fully conceded to the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor]. In 1894 preparations were 
2 CONC made for the celebration of [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]'s sixtieth birthday, and officials and men of wealth were invited to contribute funds for the occasion. Officially, at least 1,206,900 ta
2 CONC els were received—unofficial contributions doubtless being much greater. Even the outbreak of the Sino-Japanese War did not prevent the preparations from going on, but when reports of continual defeats came to Peking [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] reluctantly cancelled the event. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the next four years friction between the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] and [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] increased. Under the tutorship of [http
2 CONC s://www.geni.com/people/WTH/6000000018571653918 Wêng T'ung-ho] [q.v.]—which was abolished, however, by [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] after 1896—and later with the encouragement of [https://www.ge
2 CONC ni.com/people/KYW/6000000050759211695 K'ang Yu-wei] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/TST/6000000069582008822 T'an Ssû-t'ung]) and others, the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] became convinced of 
2 CONC the necessity of specific reform measures which, by the summer of 1898, he was eager to put into effect. But actual power was still in the hands of conservative officials who, fearful of losing their posts, rallied to the support 
2 CONC of the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager]. She, too, was fully aware of the danger to her power should the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] succeed in carrying out his 
2 CONC plans, and so immediately took steps to maneuver her supporters into key positions. The loyal [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu] was made governor-general of Chihli in command of the best trained and best
2 CONC  equipped troops of the day. On June 15, 1898 [https://www.geni.com/people/WTH/6000000018571653918 Wêng] was ordered to retire and thus the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] was deprived of the last pers
2 CONC on who might have effected a reconciliation between the two factions. The only great official with military power whom the reformers believed they could hold to their side was [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/6000000011573123374 Y
2 CONC üan Shih-k'ai] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YJS/6000000012675548407 Yüan Chia-san]) but in the end he betrayed them by revealing the plans to the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager]. On 
2 CONC September 22 the latter summoned the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] to her presence and ordered him placed in confinement. Once more she assumed full powers as regent, keeping at her side all the reac
2 CONC tionary and corrupt officials and eunuchs who certainly would have been removed if the reform movement of that summer had succeeded. Six of the leading reformers were executed, others found refuge in foreign countries, and the res
2 CONC t were cashiered (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/TST/6000000069582008822 T'an Ssû-t'ung]). Although [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] and her followers thus completely wrecked the [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor]'s projected reforms they were unsuccessful in their plan to dethrone him. Early in 1900 [https://www.geni.com/people/PJ/6000000009824682318 P'u-chun], son of [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] (for both see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009824088033 I-tsung]), was made Heir Apparent as a preliminary step to dethronement. Congratulations to [https://www.geni.com/people
2 CONC /ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] from the foreign ministers were then in order, but when these were not forthcoming his pride was piqued and from then on he lent his hand to anti-foreign activities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Late in 1889 the secret society known as I-ho ch'üan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/RL/6000000009796689882 Jung-lu]), or Boxers, became active in Shantung province, but when [https://www.geni.com/people/YSK/60000000115731
2 CONC 23374 Yüan Shih-k'ai] was made governor the agitators were driven to Chihli and Shansi, and by May 1900 anti-foreign riots and the destruction of churches became ominous in those provinces. By June, with the connivance of [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/%E7%AB%AF%E9%83%A1%E7%8E%8B-%E8%BC%89%E6%BC%AA-%E4%BA%8C/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] and other high officials, the Boxers entered Peking and on the 11th of that month unruly Kansu soldiers, who earlier had bee
2 CONC n brought in to guard the city, killed a secretary of the Japanese Legation. The foreign ministers took steps to guard the Legation Quarter and asked the assistance of their governments for troops. On the 17th the Allied Forces to
2 CONC ok the Taku forts. On the 19th the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824253093 Tsai-i] overruled all opposition, and a manifesto was issued asked
2 CONC  all foreign envoys to leave Peking within twenty-four hours under the escort of Chinese troops. On the following day the German Minister, [https://www.geni.com/people/Clemens-von-Ketteler-freiherr/6000000019860817999 Baron von Ke
2 CONC ttler], was murdered by soldiers who had [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY6000000009824253093 Tsai-i]'s orders to kill any foreigner at sight. Thus began the siege of the foreign communities in Peking. One group of 473 foreign civil
2 CONC ians, some 400 guards, and several thousand Chinese converts and servants barricaded themselves inside the Legation Quarter, and a similar but smaller group defended itself in the Pei T'ang 北堂, the Catholic Cathedral, in another p
2 CONC art of the city. Rioters burned and pillaged in many places-not even the homes of princes and high officials were spared. In Shansi province more than a hundred foreigners were killed and many others, including Chinese Christians,
2 CONC  suffered cruelly. That the ravages did not spread to central and south China was due chiefly to the efforts of intelligent officials like [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZD/6000000018409429876 Chang Chih-tung], [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/LKY/6000000071236117867 Liu K'un-i] and [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] [qq.v.]. On August 14, the Allied Relief Expedition entered Peking and the siege of the Legations and the Cathedr
2 CONC al was lifted. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Very early the next morning the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] fled from the capital with the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor], a few officials, and servants. After
2 CONC  many deprivations the party reached Taiyuan, Shansi, on September 10; and Sian, Shênsi, on October 26. On the way a number of edicts were issued in the name of the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] in w
2 CONC hich he and his officials were made to take the blame for the disasters that had overtaken the Empire. Thus did [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] clear herself of responsibility and cannily p
2 CONC repare for return to power when the time was ripe. The Court remained at Sian for more than a year, entrusting to [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] and to [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/600000000
2 CONC 9796754104 I-k'uang] the difficult task of negotiating with the Powers. There was little these representatives could do except to agree to the demands incorporated in a protocol signed on September 7, 1901. While still at Sian the
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] began to issue decrees—in reply to demands of [https://www.geni.com/people/LKY/6000000071236117867 Liu K'un-i] and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZD/600000001840
2 CONC 9429876 Chang Chih-tung]—looking toward various political and social reforms. Her belated interest in these matters may properly be interpreted as gestures to win the approval of foreigners and the support of the governors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 7, 1902 the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager], the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] and their entourage returned to Peking. Her attitude toward foreigners w
2 CONC as now one of gratitude for having spared her from deserved humiliation and for allowing her to return to power. She gave many receptions to foreign diplomats and their wives, to missionaries and tourists, and soon won many friend
2 CONC s by her cordial and charming manner. The reforms in government which she decreed were nor essentially those which the [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor] had sought in 1898. The most far-reaching of these
2 CONC  was the abolition of the old style examinations in 1905; others concerned the establishment of modern schools and the sending of a few students abroad for study, particularly in Japan. A decree was issued allowing inter-marriage 
2 CONC between Manchus and Chinese, but when a Cabinet was formed in 1906 it was composed of nine bannermen and only four Chinese, thus actually very little was done to mitigate the growing animosity between the two races. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 14, 1908 [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] died, and on the following day the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] who had dominated his entire career
2 CONC , also died. Before her death she named as the next emperor, [https://www.geni.com/people/PY/6000000009796535956 P'u-i] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien]), son of [https://www.geni.com/peop
2 CONC le/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung]'s brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZF/6000000009796382188 Tsai-fêng] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]). He was to carry on the line of both [ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Mu-tsung] and [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tê-tsung], but as he was then only three ''sui'', [https://www.geni.com/people/ZF/6000000009796382188 Tsai-fêng] wa
2 CONC s appointed Prince Regent. The posthumous name, '''Hsiao-chin Hsien Huang-hou''', was given the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager], and her remains were buried in the Eastern Mausoleum. Her tablet
2 CONC  was placed in the Imperial Ancestral Hall. The close proximity of the deaths of [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor Tê-tsung] ga
2 CONC ve rise to many suspicions as to the manner of the latter's decease. Whether he died a natural death, or was murdered, has never been determined. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]'s appointment of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZF/6000000009796382188 Tsai-fêng] as regent, and his son as heir to the throne, was not made out of consideration for t
2 CONC he security of the dynasty or the welfare of the Empire. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZF/6000000009796382188 Tsai-fêng] was lacking in nearly every quality necessary to a Prince Regent, as shown by his inability to restrain his br
2 CONC others and other high princes when they forced him to appoint them to high positions in the government, irrespective of their qualifications. He thus lost the support of many able Chinese officials who might otherwise have come to
2 CONC  his aid when the revolution broke out in 1911. Having yielded at every point on essential matters he had finally to yield to the abdication of his son and to the extinction of the dynasty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The career of [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] in the Palace began in 1860 and ended with her death in 1908. For thirty-seven of those years she ruled the Palace and those nearest her with v
2 CONC irtually absolute power, and for eleven years she ruled indirectly—a total of forty-eight years. Her outstanding endowments were an unquenchable ambition, a love of power, a love of money, and a physical vitality which almost neve
2 CONC r failed. She knew both the strength and weaknesses of men in high places; tactfully she used their talents to carry out great policies, and did not scruple to take advantage of their foibles for ends both selfish and cruel. She w
2 CONC as superstitious, but in matters of policy was realistic. Considering her limited advantages, she gained a broad view of Chinese literature and a good working knowledge of Chinese documentary style. She was interested in music and
2 CONC  art, and the theatre owed much to her patronage. Her calligraphy was better than average and she could also paint. After the Boxer turmoil she took sufficient interest in Western customs and modes of social intercourse to appoint
2 CONC , as ladies-in-waiting, two daughters of [https://www.geni.com/people/Y%C3%BC-Keng-%E8%A3%95%E5%BA%9A/6000000013707497434 Yü-kêng 裕庚] (T. 朗西, d. 1905), one-time minister to Japan (1895-98) and to France (1899-1902). These young wo
2 CONC men knew several foreign languages and served as interpreters when the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] entertained Western guests. One of them, known as [https://www.geni.com/people/Der-Ling-Li
2 CONC zzie/6000000002666054499 "Princess" Der Ling], wrote several accounts of her experiences in the Palace, giving interesting sidelights on the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager] and her Court. 
2 CONT
