1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''I-hsin''' 奕訢 (H. 鑑園主人, 樂道主人), Jan. 11, 1833-1898, May 29, the first '''Prince Kung''' (恭親王), was the sixth son of [https://www.geni.com/people/DG/6000000008737149905 Emperor Hsüan-tsung] (see under Min-ning). Hi
2 CONC s mother, [https://www.geni.com/people/XJ/6000000009797288448 Empress Hsiao-ching 孝靜成皇后], (née Borjigit, 1812-1855), a concubine, was kind by nature and cared for the [https://www.geni.com/people/DG/6000000008737149905 Emperor]'s 
2 CONC fourth son, [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 I-chu] [q.v.], after the latter's own mother had died (1840). Thus [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 I-chu] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000
2 CONC 000009796676885 I-hsin] were brought up together and became very friendly. After [https://www.geni.com/people/DG/6000000008737149905 Emperor Hsüan-tsung] died (1850), [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 I-chu (Empe
2 CONC ror Wên-tsung)] succeeded to the throne, and conferred on [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s mother the rank of Empress Dowager. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By his father's will, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was made in 1850 a prince of the first degree with the designation, Kung. In 1852 he was given a palace of his own (see under [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]). When the Taiping expedition to the north threatened Peking in 1853 [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was one of the princes in charge of patrolling the Metropolit
2 CONC an area. Later in the same year (1853) he was made a Grand Councilor and in 1854 was given the concurrent posts of lieutenant-general of a Banner and presiding controller of the Imperial Clan Court. In August 1855, when his mother
2 CONC  died, he was severely reprimanded by [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung] for negligence in the observance of the mourning ceremonies. Whatever may have been the real cause of the dispute between
2 CONC  them, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was deprived of all his posts and was ordered to study once more in the Palace School for Princes. In 1857 he was reappointed lieutenant-general of a Banner and tw
2 CONC o years later was named a senior chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time the forces of the Western powers who were invading China were approaching Peking. In 1858 the British and French troops reached Tientsin where they obtained virtually everything they demanded. The treaties of Tientsin
2 CONC  were signed by [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s father-in-law, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] [q.v.]. During this crisis [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/60000000097966
2 CONC 76885 I-hsin] pointed out in a memorial that of all the demands of the Allies the most harmful to China was the opening of the Yangtze River to foreigners. He proposed retaliation against the British interpreter, [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/Lay/6000000018460574100 H. N. Lay] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/QY/6000000019740854038 Ch'i-ying]), and also preparation for war. Later he headed the commission which conducted the trial of [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/QY/6000000019740854038 Ch'i-ying] [q.v.]. The commissioners asked lenient treatment for the aged diplomat, but the request was denied. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1860 the allied forces reappeared at Tientsin to avenge the defeat of the previous year (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in]). [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/60000000113946
2 CONC 79325 Kuei-liang] was again sent to negotiate with them, but he could not prevent their marching on to Tungchow. From September 8 to 21 the task of making peace with the invaders was entrusted to [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/60
2 CONC 00000009824379253 Tsai-yüan] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009824053557 Yin-hsiang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun]). But on September 18 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000
2 CONC 009824379253 Tsai-yüan] and [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in], acting on orders from [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsung], held as prisoners the British secr
2 CONC etaries, [https://www.geni.com/people/Parkes/6000000017337769811 Harry S. Parkes] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YMC/6000000075407620938 Yeh Ming-ch'ên]) and [https://www.geni.com/people/Henry-Brougham-Loch-1st-Baron-Loch
2 CONC -GCB-GCMG/6000000001744265100 Henry B. Loch] (1827-1900), who had been sent as negotiators. In all, twenty-six British and thirteen Frenchmen were seized. This spurred the allies to fight, and on September 21 they won the battle o
2 CONC f Pa-li-ch'iao (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in]), thus preparing the way for the advance on Peking. On the 22nd [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor Wên-tsu
2 CONC ng] fled from the Summer Palace (Yüan-ming Yüan) to Jehôl. On the preceding day he had entrusted to [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] the onerous task of making peace with the allies. On the one hand the 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor] continued to issue positive orders, as for example the execution of [https://www.geni.com/people/Parkes/6000000017337769811 Parkes]; and on the other hand the allies ins
2 CONC isted on the release of the prisoners as a condition of peace. Though the allies were then short of ammunition and supplies, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] and [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000
2 CONC 017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in] were not cognizant of that fact and worked on the supposition that they must negotiate peace. After the allies had obtained the necessary supplies they advanced on Peking, reached the Yuan-ming Yüan
2 CONC  on September 26, and for three days pillaged the Summer Palace. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] who had up to this time been living in his garden near the Summer Palace now fled to Lu-kou-ch'iao 蘆溝橋. T
2 CONC he city of Peking was thus left to the mercy of the allies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], of course, was in a precarious position. He had no soldiers under his command and only a few officials remained to assist him. Moreover, never having had contact with fo
2 CONC reigners, he had to rely on the advice of [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and Hêng-ch'i 恆祺 (T. 子久, posthumous name 勤敏, 1802?-1867, Jan. 30). In fact it was Hêng-ch'i who carried on the negotiations 
2 CONC with the British and who on October 8 effected the release of the prisoners who survived. The Allies entered Peking on October 13 and, in retaliation for the death of thirteen British and eight French prisoners, burned the Yüan-mi
2 CONC ng Yüan (October 18). In the meantime [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] had moved on the 14th to the monastery, T'ien-ning-ssû 天寧寺, west of Peking, in order to be nearer the city. On October 24 he entered
2 CONC  the city, exchanged with [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Bruce-8th-Earl-of-Elgin/6000000007728464345 Lord Elgin] the texts of the British Treaty of Tientsin and signed the Convention of Peking in nine articles. The next day th
2 CONC e French treaty of ten articles was signed, and the texts of the Treaty of Tientsin were exchanged. The Conventions of Peking of 1860 guaranteed permanent residence for foreign envoys in the capital, named Tientsin as a treaty por
2 CONC t, and granted an increase in indemnities. The British obtained the lease of Kowloon, and the French a promise that all the confiscated property of Catholic missions in China would be restored to the owners. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the crisis of 1860 Russia took the part of peacemaker. Her envoy, General Ignatieff (see under I-shan), asserted that the peace was made possible by his efforts, and so obtained from [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/600000000979
2 CONC 6676885 I-hsin] the signature (November 14) of the Russian treaty of fifteen articles by which China ceded to her all the territory east of the Ussuri River. The demarcation of the boundaries took place in 1861 (see under I-shan).
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], as signer of these treaties, became responsible for their execution. In his previous contact with foreigners he had shown toward them an attitude of disdain mingled with
2 CONC  hatred and fear. But after the conclusion of the Conventions of Peking in 1860 his attitude seems to have changed. As he understood the British better he began to show appreciation of them, if not outright admiration. His confirm
2 CONC ation of [https://www.geni.com/people/Lay/6000000018460574100 H. N. Lay] as inspector-general of customs and the trust he put in [https://www.geni.com/people/Lay/6000000018460574100 Lay]'s successor, [https://www.geni.com/people/H
2 CONC art/6000000013715665097 Robert Hart] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZD/6000000018409429876 Chang Chih-tung]), are indications of his altered point of view. On January 20, 1861 [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/600000000979
2 CONC 6676885 I-hsin]'s proposal to establish an office to take charge of foreign affairs was approved by the [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor] with the result that the Tsung-li ko-kuo shih-wu ya-mên 總理各國事務衙門,
2 CONC  commonly abbreviated as Tsungli Yamen, came into existence, and for some forty years played an important role in the modernization of China. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was placed in charge of this
2 CONC  new organization with [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/WX/6000000049172096927 Wên-hsiang] [q.v.] as his assistants. In order to train young men as interpreters, the 
2 CONC Tung-wên kuan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/DX/6000000058335104062 Tung Hsün] and Li Shan-lan) was established in 1862 as a subordinate office to the Tsungli Yamen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Allied troops evacuated Peking, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] repeatedly requested his half-brother, the [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor], to return to the city, 
2 CONC but the latter preferred to remain away and so avoid embarrassing audiences with foreign envoys. In addition, the [https://www.geni.com/people/XF/6000000008737283555 Emperor] shrank from viewing the ruins of his once lovely garden
2 CONC s. In August 1861 he died, having willed his throne to his son ([https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung]), with a regency during the son's minority, composed of the two Dowager Empresses, [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/XZ/6000000009796838324 Hsiao-chên], and eight officials (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]). But the emp
2 CONC resses found the eight officials intractable and so conspired with [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] to remove them.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November a new arrangement was made by which the empresses acted as co-regents and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] as I-chêng Wang 議政王 or Prince Counselor (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6
2 CONC 000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in] and [https://www.geni.com/people/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun]). [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] declined an offer to make his princedom "perpetually inheritable" (世襲),
2 CONC  but accepted the double annual stipend of a prince of the first degree. In addition to directing foreign affairs he was in charge of the Grand Council which advised the throne on all important matters of state; had supervision of
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung]'s education; and took charge of the Peking Field Force (Shên-chi-ying 神機營), a division of musketeers organized in 1862 with firearms presented by Russia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1864 Nanking was fully recovered from the Taipings (see under Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan). [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was lauded for his part in directing the campaign and was rewarded by being given the 
2 CONC additional rank of a prince of the third degree which was inherited by his eldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZC/6000000009824671722 Tsai-ch'êng] (see below). Nevertheless, after foreign pressure had eased, and the civil war
2 CONC  was nearly over, the powers of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] were found to be too great for the comfort of the ambitious [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in]. On A
2 CONC pril 2, 1865 he was deprived of all his offices on the vague charge that he had shown partiality to his relatives and was often careless in his conduct at Court. Though, owing to the urgent pleas of officials at Court, he was rein
2 CONC stated as head of the Tsungli Yamen (April 7) and of the Grand Council (May 8), he no longer held the rank of Prince Counselor. In 1872, when [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor Mu-tsung] married, he confer
2 CONC red on [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s princedom the right of perpetual inheritance. Nevertheless, after the [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor] took over in 1873 the control 
2 CONC which previously rested with the dowager empresses, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was not always in favor. By September 10, 1874 [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] had so disp
2 CONC leased the [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor] that he was deprived of all his ranks and offices and was reduced to a prince of the second degree. His son, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZC/60000000098246717
2 CONC 22 Tsai-ch'êng], was at the same time deprived of his rank as a prince of the third degree. The edict accused [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] of discourteous conduct in an audience. But the real cause w
2 CONC as a dispute on the question of the restoration of the Yüan-ming Yüan, which was opposed by [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]. Nevertheless, on the following day the [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/600000
2 CONC 0008737146186 Emperor], by order of the dowager empresses, and at the request of princes and high officials, was compelled to restore to [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] and to [https://www.geni.com/peop
2 CONC le/ZC/6000000009824671722 Tsai-ch'êng] all their ranks and offices. It is said that the decision to retain [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was in some way motivated by the strained relations then existi
2 CONC ng between China and Japan over the murder of Loochoo Islanders in Formosa (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SBZ/6000000026592944887 Shên Pao-chên]). Late in 1874, when [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emp
2 CONC eror Mu-tsung] had 'temporarily recovered from smallpox, he dispensed various honors to courtiers and at the same time had [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s stipends tripled. In the course of the [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor]'s illness [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was entrusted with reading and answering memorials. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the [https://www.geni.com/people/TZ/6000000008737146186 Emperor]'s death, it would have been a simple matter to curb the ambition of [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] by choosing as Emp
2 CONC eror a more mature person. But the opportunity was lost when [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] named her own nephew, [https://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Tsai-t'ien] [q.v.], th
2 CONC e son of [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan] [q.v.], successor to the throne (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Hsiao-ch'in]). The power of [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/600000
2 CONC 0009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] was thus firmly established. Although [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] continued in office nine years longer (1875-84), his authority gradually diminished. In 1884, whe
2 CONC n war with France seemed inevitable, irresponsible censors, particularly [https://www.geni.com/people/SY/6000000010531275071 Shêng-yü] [q.v.], blamed him for mismanagement of the government. Consequently he and all the members of 
2 CONC the Tsungli Yamen and the Grand Council were cashiered or degraded. [https://www.geni.com/people/SD/6000000009823407697 Shih-to] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZL/6000000009822745089 Chao-lien]) was given charge of the Gr
2 CONC and Council (until 1901) and [https://www.geni.com/people/YK/6000000009796754104 I-k'uang] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]) directed the Tsungli Yamen. But the real power rested with the [h
2 CONC ttps://www.geni.com/people/GX/6000000009796512974 Emperor]'s father, [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan], until the latter died early in 1891. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus after having steered the country on a safe course for twenty-three years, the experience of [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] was disregarded. He was allowed to retain his princedom as a perpetual in
2 CONC heritance, but was deprived of all his offices and of his added annual stipends. In 1886 he was again honored by being given double stipends, but remained in obscurity. To be sure, in the crisis of the Sino-Japanese War, he u as a
2 CONC gain called upon to serve the country, for in October 1894 he was once more placed in charge of the Tsungli Yamen and was ordered to serve on the Board of Admiralty (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-
2 CONC huan]) and on the War Council (Chün-wu ch'u 軍務處), an office especially created to direct the war with Japan. And in December he was made head of the Grand Council. Nevertheless, the war was already lost, and there was nothing [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] could do except to witness the conclusion of the ignominious Treaty of Shimonoseki in 1895 (see under Li Hung-chang). Moreover, he was old and infirm and was filled with rese
2 CONC ntment against the entire Court. He declined to assume full responsibility and spent most of his last days at his garden, Lang-jun Yüan 朗潤園, west of Peking and north of the present campus of Yenching University. In February 1898 h
2 CONC e was made acting presiding controller of the Imperial Clan Court, but he died soon after. His passing was mourned by [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] and he was eulogized for his great cont
2 CONC ributions to the empire. He was canonized as '''Chung''' 忠 (Loyal) and his name was celebrated in the Imperial Ancestral Temple and in the Temple of Eminent Statesmen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] had four sons but the two younger ones died young. The eldest, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZC/6000000009824671722 Tsai-ch'êng 載澂] (posthumous name 果敏, 1858-1885), was ma
2 CONC de (1862) a prince of the third degree, but left no male heir. The second, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824505963 Tsai-ying 載瀅] (T. 湛甫 H. 雲林居士, b. 1861), was for a time the adopted son of his uncle, I-ho (see under Mi
2 CONC n-ning), and inheritor of a third-degree princedom. In 1900 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824505963 Tsai-ying] was deprived of all his ranks because of his pro-Boxer activities. Early in 1897, by order of [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in], [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000009824505963 Tsai-ying]'s eldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/PW/6000000009824841337 P'u-wei 溥偉] (T. 紹原), was appointed the a
2 CONC dopted son of the long deceased [https://www.geni.com/people/ZC/6000000009824671722 Tsai-ch'êng]. Thus in 1898 [https://www.geni.com/people/PW/6000000009824841337 P'u-wei] inherited the princedom and became the second Prince Kung.
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s eldest daughter, [https://www.geni.com/people/RS/6000000009847050628 Princess Jung-shou 榮壽公主] (1854-1911), was adopted by [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796
2 CONC 777603 Empress Hsiao-ch'in] (probably early in 1862) and was brought up in the Palace. In 1866 she married [https://www.geni.com/people/ZR/6000000009847039490 Chih-tuan] 志端 (d. 1871), a descendant of Ming-jui [q.v.]. After her hus
2 CONC band's death [https://www.geni.com/people/RS/6000000009847050628 Princess Jung-shou] lived in the Palace as adopted daughter and companion to the [https://www.geni.com/people/CX/6000000009796777603 Empress Dowager]. In 1881 she wa
2 CONC s given the privilege of riding in a yellow sedan chair with imperial equipage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], as founder of the Tsungli Yamen and as its head for twenty-seven years (1861-84, 1894-98), conducted China's foreign affairs on a basis of conciliation. Owing perhaps to
2 CONC  the experience he had gained in 1860, he knew what it meant to be defeated, to face internal rebellion and foreign invasion with no troops he could rely on, attended moreover by officials who gave him only half-hearted support. H
2 CONC e had learned to make all necessary concessions in order to maintain peace. The less enlightened courtiers could not understand him and attacked him on the ground that he was weak. After his removal in 1884 China entered unprepare
2 CONC d on the path of war and soon found herself embroiled with France and, ten years later, with Japan. When he was called on in 1894 to direct the Sine-Japanese war it was already too late. Had he been in power during the years 1884 
2 CONC and 1894, the disputes with France and Japan might have taken different courses. In any case he probably would have prevented the Empress Dowager from spending on the Summer Palace funds meant for the navy, and kept her from other
2 CONC  follies which directly or indirectly led to the disasters of 1900. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] left two collections of verse: one entitled 樂道堂詩集 ''Lo-tao t'ang shih-chi'', another 萃錦吟 ''Ts'ui-chin-yin''. The former is in six series, each with its own title, various
2 CONC ly printed from 1856 to 1867. The latter, in 18 ''chüan'', was printed about 1893. His prose collection is entitled ''Lo-tao t'ang wen'' (文) ''chi'', 5 ''chüan''. He headed the commissions for the compilation of the following two 
2 CONC official works, both completed in 1872: 剿平粵匪方略 ''Chiao-p'ing Yüeh-fei fang-lüeh'', 420 + 2 ''chüan'', containing edicts and memorials relating to the Taiping Rebellion in the years 1850-66; and ''Chiao-p'ing Nien'' (捻) ''fei fang-
2 CONC lüeh'', 320 + 1 ''chüan'', relating to the suppression of the Nien bandits during the years 1851-68. The former should not be confused with the ''P'ing-ting'' (平定) ''Yüeh-fei chi'' (紀) ''lüeh'', 18 + 4 ''chüan'', printed about 186
2 CONC 5 by Kuan-wên [q.v.]. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] also supervised the editing of the official documents in these works which were completed in 1896, namely: 平定雲南回匪方略 ''P'ing-ting Yün-nan Hui fei fan
2 CONC g-lüeh'', 50 ''chüan'', concerning the Mohammedan uprising in Yunnan (see under Ts'en Yü-ying) between 1855 and 1879; 平定貴州苗匪紀略 ''P'ing-ting Kuei-chou Miao fei chi-lüeh'', 40 ''chüan'', on wars with the Miao in the years 1855-79; a
2 CONC nd ''P'ing-ting Shan, Kan, Hsin-chiang'' (陝甘新疆) ''Hui-fei fang-lüeh'', 320 ''chüan'', on the Mohammedan rebellions in the northwestern provinces (see under Tso Tsung-t'ang) from 1855 to 1889. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/600000
2 CONC 0009796676885 I-hsin]'s own memorials appear in these works and also in the collections of documents relating to foreign affairs in the Hsien-fêng and T'ung-chih periods, known as: 籌辦夷務始末 ''Ch'ou-pan I-wu shih-mo'', ''Hsien-fêng c
2 CONC h'ao'', 80 ''chuan'', completed in 1867, covering the years 1850-61; and the ''Ch'ou-pan I-wu shih-mo'', ''T'ung-chih ch'ao'', 100 ''chüan'', completed in 1880, covering the years 1861-75. These two works, together with the ''Ch'o
2 CONC u-pan i-wu shih-mo'', ''Tao-kuang ch'ao'', 80 ''chüan'', covering the years 1836-50, were printed by the Palace Museum, Peking, in 1929-31. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]'s memorials also appear in the
2 CONC  清季外交史料 ''Ch'ing-chi wai-chiao shih-liao'', documents on foreign relations in the last two reigns (1875-1911) of the Ch'ing dynasty, compiled by Wang Yen-wei 王彥威 (T. 弢夫 H. 藜盦, original ''ming'' 禹堂 T. 渠城 Jan, 1843-1904) and printed
2 CONC  in 1932-35. Appended to the compilation is Wang's work about affairs in 1900-02, entitled 西巡大事記 ''Hsi-hsün ta-shih chi'', 11+ 1 ''chüan''. 
2 CONT
