1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yung-lin''' 永璘, June 17, 1766-1820, Apr. 25, the first '''Prince Ch'ing''' (慶親王), was the seventeenth son of Emperor Kao-tsung (see under Hung-li). His mother was Empress Hsiao-i and he was a younger brother of 
2 CONC Emperor Jên-tsung (for both see under Yung-yen). In 1789 he was made a prince of the third degree and in 1799, after his brother ascended the throne, he was made a prince of the second degree with the designation '''Hui''' (惠郡王) w
2 CONC hich a few days later was changed to Ch'ing. After the property of [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] [q.v.] was confiscated, [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin] was given the en
2 CONC ormous palace which [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] had occupied. It is said that in the last years of the Ch'ien-lung period (1736-96) several princes deliberated on methods of getting rid of [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên], and that whereas other princes, including Yung-yen, expressed decisive views on the matter, [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin] said nothing. When chi
2 CONC ded for his silence he is said to have remarked that, unlike his brothers, he had no ambition in political matters, but would accept a gift of [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên]'s magnificent residence in
2 CONC  Peking. However this may be, when [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] was removed, Emperor Jên-tsung saw to it that the wish of [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin] was fulfilled.
2 CONC  About the year 1852 this palace, situated northeast of the present Catholic University, became the property of I-hsin [q.v.]. [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin] was a man of mediocre ability and never p
2 CONC layed a conspicuous role. When he became ill in 1820 the Emperor paid him a visit and elevated him to a prince of the first degree (親王). He died shortly thereafter and was canonized as '''Hsi''' (僖). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of his sons grew to maturity. The third, Mien-min 綿愍 (d. 1836, posthumous name 良), inherited the reduced rank of a prince of the second degree and became the second Prince Ch'ing. The other two, Mien-t'i 綿悌, (d. 1849) and Mi
2 CONC en-hsing 綿性, were evidently out of favor with Emperor Hsüan-tsung, for when Mien-min died without a male heir, a grand-nephew of [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin], named I-ts'ai (see under Yung-hsüan), 
2 CONC was appointed his successor. Out of deference to Mien-min, Emperor Hsüan-tsung allowed I-ts'ai to inherit the princedom of the second degree instead of one of a lower rank. But in 1842 I-ts'ai was accused of having taken a concubi
2 CONC ne during a mourning period, and of having bribed a clerk in the Imperial Clan Court to have his punishment minimized. In the meantime Mien-hsing himself, hoping to inherit I-ts'ai's rank, bribed a clerk to bring to view additiona
2 CONC l misdemeanors of I-ts'ai in order that the latter might be wholly discredited. Finally all involved were punished. I-ts'ai was deprived of all ranks, and Mien-hsing, and the clerks who were implicated, were sent into exile. Mien-
2 CONC t'i was then appointed heir to [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]'s dwindling estate. It seems that about this time (1842) the descendants of [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]
2 CONC  had to leave their lavish palace and were given the confiscated residence of [https://www.geni.com/people/QS/6000000011384255255 Ch'i-shan] [q.v.] on the street named Ting-fu ta-chieh 定府大街. Whatever the original condition of this
2 CONC  residence, it later became one of the richest in Peking, and in the last years of the dynasty was a political center. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mien-t'i died without a male heir, and a son of Mien-hsing named I-k'uang 奕劻 (1836-1916), ...
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/171/18b; 1/227/5a; ''Chin-shih jên-wu chih'' (see under Wêng T'ung-ho) p. 219; ''T'oung Pao'', 1916, p. 393; ''T'ien-chih t'u-wên'' (see bibl. under Pao-ting) 4/25a.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
