1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Kuei-liang''' 桂良 (T. 燕山), Sept. 9, 1785-1862, July 17, official, was a Manchu of the Guwalgiya Clan and a member of the Plain Red Banner. His father, [https://www.geni.com/people/YD/6000000011518489665 Yü-tê 玉德]
2 CONC  (T. 達齋 H. 他山, d. 1809), served as governor-general of Fukien and Chekiang from 1799 to 1806, but was banished for negligence in conducting the war against the pirates (see under Li Ch'ang-kêng). In 1808 [https://www.geni.com/peop
2 CONC le/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang], then a senior licentiate, purchased the rank of a second class secretary of a Board, and later was assigned to the Board of Ceremonies. Also by purchase, he attained (1814) the rank of assist
2 CONC ant department director in the same Board. Five years later he was sent to Szechwan where he served until 1827, first as a prefect, and then as intendant of the Chien-ch'ang Circuit. From 1827 to 1834 he acted as financial commiss
2 CONC ioner of the following provinces: Szechwan (1829-30), Kwangtung (1830-32), and Kiangsi (1832-34). In 1834 he was made governor of Honan where he destroyed (1838) the temples which had been erected in the province by certain rebell
2 CONC ious religious sects. He was made governor-general of Hunan and Hupeh in 1839 but later was transferred to be governor-general of Fukien and Chekiang, and early in the following year was given the same post in Yunnan and Kweichow.
2 CONC  In 1845 he went to Peking for an audience after which [https://www.geni.com/people/Minning/6000000008737149905 Emperor Hsüan-tsung] remarked that [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang], who was then just 
2 CONC over sixty ''sui'', showed signs of impaired health, which rendered him unfit for the office of governor-general. He was ordered to await an appointment in Peking. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Late in 1845 [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was named military lieutenant-governor of Jehôl where he served until 1848 when he was recalled to Peking to look after affairs in connection with the im
2 CONC minent marriage of his tenth daughter (1834-1880) to the emperor's sixth son, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] [q.v.]. Thereafter [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] held seve
2 CONC ral minor posts in Peking until 1851 when he was sent to Foochow as the Tartar General of that city. He was recalled in 1852 and named president of the Board of War. In the following year he was sent to Paoting, capital of Chihli,
2 CONC  to take charge of the defenses against invasion by the Taiping rebels. In the same year he was appointed governor-general of Chihli, and two years later was given the concurrent rank of an Associate Grand Secretary. Early in 1857
2 CONC  he was promoted to be a Grand Secretary and served concurrently as lieutenant-general of the Manchu Plain White Banner. His post of governor-general of Chihli was taken by [https://www.geni.com/people/TTX/6000000019472789281 T'an
2 CONC  T'ing-hsiang 譚廷襄] (T. 竹厓 H. 竹巖, posthumous name 端恪, ''chin-shih'' of 1833, d. 1870). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1854, while in Chihli, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] had his first experience in the conduct of foreign affairs. In that year the British, French, and American envoys landed at Taku to request 
2 CONC a revision of their treaties with China (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/I-chu/6000000008737283555 I-chu]). [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was ordered not to demean himself by receiving the 
2 CONC envoys personally, but to send a former salt controller, Ch'ung-lun (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/I-chu/6000000008737283555 I-chu]), to discuss the matter with them at Tientsin. As the demands of the envoys were nearly a
2 CONC ll rejected, they returned south to await further instructions from their respective governments. Finally the British and the French decided to use force to obtain their objectives which in general were: the establishment of Legat
2 CONC ions in Peking on terms of diplomatic equality, the opening of the interior to foreign trade and travel, toleration for missionaries, and an extension of trading facilities. After the occupation of Canton in 1857 the British pleni
2 CONC potentiary, [https://www.geni.com/people/Lord-Elgin/6000000007728464345 Lord Elgin], and the French Ambassador, Baron Groe (for both see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YMC/6000000075407620938 Yeh Ming-ch'ên]), sailed northward
2 CONC  with their fleets. At Shanghai they were joined, not for military but for diplomatic action, by the American envoy, [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Bradford-Reed/334110251620003405 William Bradford Reed] (1806-1876), and the
2 CONC  Russian Admiral, Count Evfīmiĭ Vasīl'evīch Putiatīn (1803-1883). In April 1858 the four arrived at Taku and demanded that [https://www.geni.com/people/TTX/6000000019472789281 T'an T'ing-hsiang] be given full powers to sign the re
2 CONC vised treaties. After s month of fruitless discussion the British and French allies forced their way to Tientsin, and threatened to proceed to Peking if high officials were not sent immediately to open negotiations. Thus [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and a Mongol, [https://www.geni.com/people/HSN/6000000011302032657 Hua-sha-na 花沙納] (T. 毓仲 H. 松岑 posthumous name 文定, 1806-1859, ''chin-shih'' of 1832), were chosen as the Emper
2 CONC or's delegates to negotiate with the plenipotentiaries of the four powers. They arrived at Tientsin on June 2, but were rebuffed by the foreign envoys for failure to provide themselves with seals. In China an official sent from th
2 CONC e capital on short errands was not given a seal: therefore a delay occurred while special seals were made for the negotiators. There was no negotiation, however, for the British interpreters [https://www.geni.com/people/Horatio-La
2 CONC y/6000000018460574100 H. N. Lay] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/QY/6000000019740854038 Ch'i-ying]) and [https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Wade/6000000010882546514 Thomas Wade] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Zuo-
2 CONC Zongtang/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang]) who conducted the meetings, brooked no mitigation of their demands. After they had frightened a third envoy away from Tientsin (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/QY/6000000019740
2 CONC 854038 Ch'i-ying]), the two interpreters had little difficulty with [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/HSN/6000000011302032657 Hua-sha-na]. The Chinese commissioners pl
2 CONC eaded with the Russian and American ministers to help alleviate the rigor of the demands, but without success. Those ministers, however, acted with more moderation and courtesy than did the British and French, probably because the
2 CONC y were in a position to obtain without force what the latter won by arms. The treaty with Russia in twelve articles was signed on June 13; the American treaty of thirty articles, on the 18th-the two interpreters who assisted Mr. [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/William-Bradford-Reed/334110251620003405 Reed] being [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Williams/6000000032086308879 S. Wells Williams] and [https://www.geni.com/people/W-A-P-Martin/6000000058335059846
2 CONC  W. A. P. Martin] (for both see under Tung Hsün). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of the British demands interposed obstacles to continued negotiation, namely, the residence of foreign envoys at Peking and the opening of the Yangtze River to foreign trade. On June 26, the day set for the signing of the Brit
2 CONC ish treaty of 56 articles, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/HSN/6000000011302032657 Hua-sha-na] were instructed by edict to refuse to concede the demands then under d
2 CONC ispute. But as the British threatened to march on Peking they forced the intimidated negotiators to sign the treaty on the evening of the same day (26th). The French treaty of 42 articles was signed on June 27. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The clause in the treaties to which the Peking Court raised the most serious objection was that which granted residence to foreign envoys in the capital on a basis of diplomatic equality. The Court was ignorant of international pr
2 CONC actices and felt humiliated at the idea of future audiences in which envoys of foreign states would not perform the ceremony of ''kowtow'' which for centuries had been the Chinese practice. But that by some of the articles China l
2 CONC ost much of her national sovereignty, no one in the Court s seemed to realize. After the foreign envoys left, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/HSN/6000000011302032657
2 CONC  Hua-sha-na] were sent to Shanghai to make agreements with representatives of the powers about the rate of tariff on various commodities. In the meantime [https://www.geni.com/people/TTX/6000000019472789281 T'an T'ing-hsiang] and 
2 CONC other officials who had charge of the defenses of Tientsin were punished for their failure to stop the "invaders" from landing. The Mongol prince, [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in] [q.v.], wa
2 CONC s entrusted with the fortifying of the Taku area and the training of troops sufficient to guard it. The emperor, still vexed at the idea of opening the Yangtze to foreign trade and at having to meet foreign envoys in Peking, instr
2 CONC ucted [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and the other commissioners to seek the annulment of those articles. In return China would abolish the tariff on all foreign goods. But finding the envoys unyie
2 CONC lding, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] sought to forestall any possible audiences by asking that the texts of the treaties be ratified, not in the capital, but at Shanghai. He warned [https://www.ge
2 CONC ni.com/people/Frederick-Bruce/6000000011335972005 Frederick Bruce] (see under Wang T'ao), the British minister, that the Taku area was fortified and that since Tientsin was not a treaty port, China had the right to resist any atte
2 CONC mpt to force a passage. However, [https://www.geni.com/people/Frederick-Bruce/6000000011335972005 Bruce] belittled the warning and with a fleet of British and French ships sailed to Taku. When told to land at Pei-t'ang, a smaller 
2 CONC port north of Taku, he refused and attacked the Taku forts (June 25, 1859). After being repulsed (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in]), the British and French envoys returned to Shang
2 CONC hai to prepare for further military action. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After this repulse the American minister, [https://www.geni.com/people/The-Hon-John-Elliott-Ward/6000000020472162910 John Elliot Ward] 華若翰 (1814-1902), and his retinue, including [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Williams/600000
2 CONC 0032086308879 S. Wells Williams] and [https://www.geni.com/people/W-A-P-Martin/6000000058335059846 W. A. P. Martin], landed at Pei-t'ang. They were conducted to the capital by [https://www.geni.com/people/CH/6000000011325508386 Ch
2 CONC 'ung-hou] [q.v.] and were quartered in a residence which had previously belonged to Sai-shang-a (see under Ch'ung-ch'i). On July 30 Ward had his first interview with [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] 
2 CONC who had by that time returned from Shanghai. Eleven days later [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] received the letter from [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Buchanan-15th-President-of-the-United-State
2 CONC s/6000000000742096365 President Buchanan] to the emperor and transmitted it to the throne. On August 16 the texts of the American treaty of 1858 were exchanged at Pei-t'ang, whereupon the Americans returned to Shanghai. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 1, 1860 the British and French allies again landed at Pei-t'ang and twenty days later took the Taku forts. As the allies reached Tientsin [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was again sent the
2 CONC re as negotiator. He was joined by the governor-general, [https://www.geni.com/people/HF/6000000011404293244 Hêng-fu 恆福] (posthumous name 恭勤 d. 1862), and the director of Imperial Armory, Hêng-ch'i (see under [https://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]), to open negotiations. They consented to every demand of the allies, and yet those allies refused for a long time to recognize them on the pretext that there had been no edict giving the Chi
2 CONC nese commissioners plenipotentiary powers. The emperor, too, reprimanded them for being too timorous. As the British advanced northward, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] returned to Tungchow and then
2 CONC  to Peking. The task of negotiating with the foreigners was first entrusted to Tsai-yüan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009824053557 Yin-hsiang]) and later to [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 
2 CONC I-hsin]. After the emperor had fled to Jehôl, [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was named one of the commissioners to conduct national affairs at Peking. He and [https://www.geni.com/people/WX/6000000
2 CONC 049172096927 Wên-hsiang] [q.v.] assisted [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin] in the negotiations with the, victorious allies-finally concluding the treaties of Peking (see under [https://www.geni.com/people
2 CONC /YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]). When in January 1861 the Tsungli Yamen was established (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]) to conduct foreign affairs, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/600000000
2 CONC 9796676885 I-hsin] was made its head with [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/WX/6000000049172096927 Wên-hsiang] as assistants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was on the side of his son-in-law, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], when the ''coup d'etat'' of November 1861 took place (see under [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]). They put into effect the edict arresting [https://www.geni.com/people/SS/6000000009823430303 Su-shun] [q.v.] and conducted the latter's trial. Along with [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin], [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang] was made a Grand Councilor. He died eight months later and was canonized as '''Wên-tuan''' 文端. He was celebrated in the Templ
2 CONC e of Eminent Statesmen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Williams/6000000032086308879 S. Wells Williams], who was secretary and interpreter of the American mission in 1858 and who negotiated with [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kue
2 CONC i-liang] the treaty of that year, gave the following description of him. "[https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kweiliang] is a well-preserved man of seventy four, tall and not too large for his height, placid in spe
2 CONC ech and countenance, having a stoop of the shoulders and a quavering tone of voice, which more than anything else indicates his age". [https://www.geni.com/people/W-A-P-Martin/6000000058335059846 W. A. P. Martin] described him as 
2 CONC "of kindly aspect and gentle demeanor; his colleague, [https://www.geni.com/people/HSN/6000000011302032657 Hua-sha-na], some twenty years his junior, had a martial air and something of the brusqueness of a soldier". 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/GL/6000000011394679325 Kuei-liang]'s elder brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/BL/6000000011518537448 Pin-liang 斌良] (T. 備卿, 笠耕, 1784-1847), served as an official for forty-five years and died at Lhas
2 CONC a a few months after he arrived there as Imperial Resident. He was a celebrated poet among the Manchus, leaving a voluminous collection of verse, entitled 抱沖齋詩集 ''Pao-ch'ung chai shih-chi'', divided into 36 sections and 71 ''chüan
2 CONC '', with a supplement of his ''tz'ǔ'', entitled 眠琴仙館詞 ''Mien-ch'in Hsien-kuan tz'ǔ'', 1 ''chüan''. It was printed in 1849-50 by a younger brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/FL/6000000013643840088 Fa-liang 法良] (T. 可盦, b. 1800), 
2 CONC who included in it a ''nien-p'u'' of [https://www.geni.com/people/BL/6000000011518537448 Pin-liang]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT __ [ 1/394/1a; 2/45/31b; ''Ch'ou-pan I-wu shih-mo'', Hsien-fêng period (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009796676885 I-hsin]); Morse, H. B., ''The International Relations of the Chinese Empire, 1834-60'' ; Cordier
2 CONC , H., ''L'expedition de Chine'', 1857-58 ; Williams, F. W., ''The Life and Letters of S. Wells Williams'' (1889), p. 265; [https://www.geni.com/people/W-A-P-Martin/6000000058335059846 Martin, W. A. P.], ''A Cycle of Cathay'', pp. 
2 CONC 143-203; 瓜爾佳氏家譜 ''Kua-êr-chia shih chia-p'u'' (1849), 4/24; Tung Hsün, ''Nien-p'u'', 2/49b.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
