1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yung-hsüan''' 永璇, Aug. 31, 1746-1832, Sept. 1, the first '''Prince I''' (儀親王), was the eighth son of [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Emperor Kao-tsung]. His mother (see under [https://www.gen
2 CONC i.com/people/YC/6000000009826099723 Yung-ch'êng]), a younger sister of [https://www.geni.com/people/JJ/6000000009826125644 Chin Chien] [q.v.], gave birth to four of [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Emperor Kao-t
2 CONC sung]'s sons, namely: [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009826099723 Yung-ch'êng] [q.v.], [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan], [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] [q.v.]
2 CONC , and one who died in infancy. From childhood [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] studied under Palace tutors, in particular, [https://www.geni.com/people/CZL/6000000049695572950 Ch'ên Chao-lun] [q.v.].
2 CONC  In 1779 he was made a prince of the second degree with the designation I (儀郡王). In the same year he and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771 Yung-hsing] were appointed directors general for the compilation of the I
2 CONC mperial Manuscript Library, ''Ssŭ-k'u ch'üan-shu'' (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/JY/6000000019201828133 Chi Yün]), and served on that Commission until the project was completed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems that [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] was never assigned to any very responsible task during his father's lifetime. In 1799, however, after [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/600000000979672278
2 CONC 0 Emperor Kao-tsung] died, the succeeding [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor Jen-tsung] immediately raised the ranks of his half brothers and nephews. [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yu
2 CONC ng-hsüan]'s princedom was elevated to the first degree and several of his sons were given minor princedoms. When the unscrupulous [https://www.geni.com/people/HS/6000000009797419358 Ho-shên] [q.v.] was imprisoned, [https://www.gen
2 CONC i.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor Jên-tsung] appointed [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] to supervise the Board of Civil Appointments, and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009825819771
2 CONC  Yung-hsing], the Board of Revenue. But later the [https://www.geni.com/people/JQ/6000000009796546943 Emperor] relieved [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] of his post as supervisor, on the ground that 
2 CONC it was not wise to concentrate too much power in the hands of a prince, and because [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] was already over-burdened with responsibilities. This was evidently the case, for 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] was at this time holding the following posts: presiding controller of the Imperial Clan Court, chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard, lieutenant general of the Manchu
2 CONC  Plain Red Banner, curator of the Imperial Library, director-general of the Board of Music, and superintendent of the affairs of two palaces. As curator of the Imperial Library, [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 
2 CONC Yung-hsüan] was in charge of the printing office, Wu-ying-tien (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/JJ/6000000009826125644 Chin Chien]). In 1809 his eldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009825401956 Mien-chih 綿志] 
2 CONC (d. 1834, posthumous name 順), was made a prince of the third degree and in 1813, for bravely resisting an uprising in Peking (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/NYC/6000000010764376232 Na-yen-ch'êng]), was given the title of a
2 CONC  prince of the second degree. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1819 [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] and his son were accused of spying in the Palace to learn in advance the nature of certain appointments. Consequently [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/60000000
2 CONC 09826162271 Yung-hsüan], then seventy-four ''sui'', was deprived of all his offices. But when [https://www.geni.com/people/DG/6000000008737149905 Min-ning] [q.v.] ascended the throne (1821) [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/60000000
2 CONC 09826162271 Yung-hsüan] was accorded the honors due his age. After his death he was canonized as '''Shên''' 慎, and [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009825401956 Mien-chih] succeeded to the reduced princedom of the second deg
2 CONC ree. The princedom was successively reduced until 1902 when [https://www.geni.com/people/MZ/6000000009825401956 Mien-chih]'s great-great-grandson, Yü-ch'i 毓岐, inherited the princedom of the fifth degree. [https://www.geni.com/peop
2 CONC le/MZ/6000000009825401956 Mien-chih]'s fifth son, [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009825896927 I-ts'ai 奕綵], was for a time (1838-42) the adopted grandson of [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin] [q.v
2 CONC .] and inherited the latter's rank of Prince Ch'ing (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/YL/6000000009797921214 Yung-lin]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009826162271 Yung-hsüan] learned to write verse from [https://www.geni.com/people/CZL/6000000049695572950 Ch'ên Chao-lun] and other eminent scholars. A manuscript copy of his collected poems,
2 CONC  entitled 古訓堂詩 ''Ku-hsin t'ang shih'', is preserved in the Library of Congress. This manuscript, in 14 volumes, contains the poems he wrote from about 1760 to 1820. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/171/12b; 1/227/3b; ''Ch'ing Huang-shih ssŭ-p'u'' (see under Fu-lung-an); ''Annual Report of the Librarian of Congress'' (1935) pp. 187-188.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
