1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yin-jêng''' 胤礽, June 16, 1674-1725, Jan. 27, one-time Heir Apparent to the throne, was the second son of [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] (see under Hsüan-yeh). His mother, 
2 CONC Empress Hsiao-ch'êng 孝誠, Feb. 4, 1654-1674), was a niece of [https://www.geni.com/people/SET/6000000010144177746 Songgotu] [q.v.]. She was married to the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] in 1665 and in 
2 CONC 1669 gave birth to a son who died in infancy. She herself died on the day that [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] was born and her death was greatly lamented by the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/600000
2 CONC 0019155600599 Emperor]. Perhaps in deference to her memory, or because she was descended from a noble family, her son, [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng], was proclaimed Heir Apparent (January 26, 1676) 
2 CONC and was brought up as such. The [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] himself taught [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] to read, and from the age of six (''sui'') onward the child 
2 CONC had for his tutors such scholarly officials as [https://www.geni.com/people/ZY/6000000019266176153 Chang Ying], [https://www.geni.com/people/LGD/6000000057094779837 Li Kuang-ti], [https://www.geni.com/people/XCL/600000001344503010
2 CONC 5 Hsiung Tzû-li] and T'ang Pin [qq.v.]. He studied both Chinese and Manchu, was an able horseman, and was skilled in the use of the bow and arrow. In the years 1696 and 1697, when the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/60000000191556
2 CONC 00599 Emperor] twice led the expeditionary force against the Eleuths (see under Fiyanggû), [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] was both times made regent to look after affairs in Peking. But even before t
2 CONC he [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] returned to the capital is 1697 he was informed that [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] was associating with men of evil character and indu
2 CONC lging in immoral practices. When the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] returned, he ordered the execution of several of the officials involved. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless the prince remained in has father's favor and was given a garden named Hsi hua-yüan 西花園, near the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor]'s own villa, Ch'ang-ch'un yüan (see under [https://www.gen
2 CONC i.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Hsüan-yeh]). When [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] set out with the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] on a projected tour of South China i
2 CONC n 1702, he fell ill at T'u-chou, Shantung, and his granduncle, [https://www.geni.com/people/SET/6000000010144177746 Songgotu], was summoned to look after him. The [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] abando
2 CONC ned the journey and returned to Peking. Before long rumors were afloat that [https://www.geni.com/people/SET/6000000010144177746 Songgotu] was too active in promoting the interests of the Heir Apparent with the consequence that in
2 CONC  1703 [https://www.geni.com/people/SET/6000000010144177746 Songgotu] was imprisoned, and there died. As [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] gradually fell into disfavor, several of his brothers begin to f
2 CONC orm cliques, in the hope of taking his place.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1708, while passing the summer in Jehôl, [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] angrily declared [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] to be a culprit, charged him with ha
2 CONC ving insulted princes and high officials, with having usurped power, and with extravagance and immorality. He also affirmed that [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] had been plotting against him and had e
2 CONC ven intended to murder him--perhaps to avenge the death of [https://www.geni.com/people/SET/6000000010144177746 Songgotu]. [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] was deprived of his position as Heir Apparent
2 CONC  and placed in confinement, but he retained the pity of his father who thought him insane. Hence when it was discovered that the eldest prince, [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000011472863175 Yin-t'i] [禔 q.v.], had employed La
2 CONC mas to cast evil spells on [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng], the latter was pardoned in 1709 and restored to his position as Heir Apparent. [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000011472863175 Yin-t'i] 
2 CONC was placed in confinement and the other princes were admonished to abandon their struggles for the throne. However, in the ensuing three years [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng]'s condition became worse 
2 CONC and the [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] abandoned hope of effecting a cure. Consequently in 1712 [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] was again degraded and placed in perpetual
2 CONC  confinement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The [https://www.geni.com/people/KX/6000000019155600599 Emperor] firmly resolved not to designate another Heir Apparent, even in defiance of the repeated requests of such high officials as [https://www.geni.com/people/ZSQ/60000000
2 CONC 49895443549 Chao Shên-ch'iao] [q.v.] in 1713, Wang Shan [q.v.] in 1717 and in 1721, and Chu T'ien-pao 朱天保 (T. 九如 (H., 鶴田, ''chin-shih'' of 1713) in 1718. For their temerity, and because they were each suspected of promoting their 
2 CONC own candidates, Wang was reprimanded and would have been banished, except for his advanced age; and Chu was executed. As to [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] he did not resign himself entirely to his fa
2 CONC te, for in 1715 it was discovered that a physician who had attended [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jeng]'s wife had acted as an intermediary in passing secret letters (written in invisible ink) between [ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] and a member of the imperial clan. The prisoner thus hoped to learn if he might be released and whether it would be possible for him to be appointed commander of the armie
2 CONC s in the northwest. The physician and others involved were severely punished. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When [https://www.geni.com/people/YZ/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] [q.v.] ascended the throne, late in 1722, he made [https://www.geni.com/people/HX/6000000009823050643 Hung-hsi 弘皙], heir of [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000
2 CONC 009822743523 Yin-jêng], a prince of the second degree with the designation Li (理郡王). [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] died in prison in 1725. He was posthumously given the rank of '''Li Ch'in-wang''' 理
2 CONC 親王 and was canonized as '''Mi''' 密. In 1728 Hung-hsi was raised to a prince of the first degree, but eleven years later was deprived of that rank by [https://www.geni.com/people/QL/6000000009796722780 Emperor Kao-tsung]. After the
2 CONC  degradation of [https://www.geni.com/people/YC/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] succeeding Ch'ing rulers declined, except for a short time in the Kuang-hsü reign-period, to announce formally the choice of an Heir Apparent. [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/YZ/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] established the practice, followed by later rulers, of placing the name of his chosen successor in a sealed box behind a tablet in the hall, Ch'ien-ch'ing kung 乾清宮, a tablet on 
2 CONC which are carved the characters, Chêng-ta kuang-ming 正大光明. The name was made public only after the Emperor's death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is worth noting that the missionary, Matteo Ripa 馬國賢 (1682-1745), was present at a scene which took place at the Ch'ang-ch'un yüan in 1712 when the princes, and the Heir Apparent in particular, were subjected to punishment. He 
2 CONC relates the incident in his Memoirs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/226/32; ''Ch'ing huang-shih ssu-pu'' (see Fu-lung-an) 3/12B; ''Tung-hua lu'', ''K'ang-hsi'' 42:5, 47:9, 48:1, 51:10; ''Memoirs of Father Ripa London'', 1855 P. 83.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
